            <ArticleTitle>Hepatocyte-Specific Deletion of BAP31 Promotes SREBP1C Activation, Promotes Hepatic Lipid Accumulation, and Worsens Insulin Resistance in Mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">jlr.M077016</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1194/jlr.M077016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Conditional knockout mice with targeted disruption of BAP31 in adult mouse liver were generated and challenged with a high-fat diet (HFD) for 36 or 96 days, markers of obesity, diabetes, and hepatic steatosis were determined. Mutant mice were indistinguishable from wild-type littermates, but exhibited increased HFD-induced obesity. BAP31-deletion in hepatocytes increased the expression of SREBP1C and the target genes, including ACC1 and SCD1, increased hepatic lipid accumulation and HFD-induced liver steatosis. Immunoprecipitation assay showed that BAP31 interacts with SREBP1C and INSIG1, and BAP31-deletion reduces INSIG1 expression, suggesting that BAP31 may regulate SREBP1C activity by modulating INSIG1 protein levels. Additionally, BAP31-deletion induced glucose and insulin intolerance, decreased Akt and Gsk3β phosphorylation, and enhanced hepatic glucose production in mice. Expression of ER stress markers was significantly induced in BAP31-mutant mice. HFD-induced inflammation was aggravated in mutant mice, along with increased JNK and NF-κB activation. These findings demonstrate that BAP31-deletion induces SREBP activation and promotes hepatic lipid accumulation, reduces insulin signaling and impairs glucose/insulin tolerance, and increases ER stress and hepatic inflammation, explaining the protective roles of BAP31 in the development of liver steatosis and insulin resistance in HFD-induced obesity in animal models.</AbstractText>
